        "starterCode": "class TreeNode {\n    int val;\n    TreeNode left;\n    TreeNode right;\n    TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\n}\n\npublic class TreeDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);\n        root.left = new TreeNode(2);\n        root.right = new TreeNode(3);\n        System.out.println(\"Root: \" + root.val + \", Left: \" + root.left.val + \", Right: \" + root.right.val);\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>⚔️ Binary Tree Topologies & Call-Stack Recursion</h2>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>💡 The Teacher's Perspective:</strong><br />\n  Welcome to hierarchical data structures! Linear arrays and linked lists are great for sequences, but the real world is organized hierarchically: file system folders, company org charts, and HTML document trees. In a <strong>Binary Tree</strong>, every node has at most two children: Left and Right!\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>🎯 What We Are Learning & Why:</strong> <em>Ascend into hierarchical data realms. Binary trees break flat linear thinking into recursive subtrees where every node is the root of its own kingdom.</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>A <strong>Binary Tree</strong> is a hierarchical data structure composed of nodes, each containing a value and references to at most two children: <code>left</code> and <code>right</code>. Unlike linked lists which have a single linear path, trees branch into recursive fractal topographies.</p>\n  <p>The call stack naturally mirrors tree topology. When you write a recursive function on a tree, each recursive invocation corresponds to descending one level deeper into the hierarchy. The maximum depth of the call stack is bounded by the height of the tree <em>H</em>: O(\\log N) in balanced trees, and O(N) in degenerate skewed trees.</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Blueprint</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\n         [ 10 (Root) ]\n           /        \\\n      [ 5 ]          [ 15 ]\n      /   \\          /    \\\n   [ 2 ]  [ 7 ]   [ 12 ]  [ 20 ]\nHeight = 3, Total Nodes = 7. Balanced: |height(left) - height(right)| <= 1\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Battle Strategy & Arena Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Base Case Discipline:</strong> Every tree recursion must start with if (root == null) return ... to terminate leaf traversal safely.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tree Height vs Depth:</strong> Depth is distance from root down to node; Height is distance from node down to its deepest descendant leaf.</li>\n  <li><strong>Degenerate Trees:</strong> A binary tree where every node has only one child degrades into a singly linked list with O(N) traversal latency.</li>\n</ul>"
