        "language": "java",
        "starterCode": "import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\npublic class TreeTraversals {\n    static class TreeNode {\n        int val; TreeNode left, right;\n        TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }\n    }\n\n    // DFS - Inorder (Left, Root, Right)\n    public static void inOrder(TreeNode root) {\n        if (root == null) return;\n        inOrder(root.left);\n        System.out.print(root.val + \" \");\n        inOrder(root.right);\n    }\n\n    // BFS - Level Order\n    public static void levelOrder(TreeNode root) {\n        if (root == null) return;\n        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        queue.offer(root);\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            TreeNode curr = queue.poll();\n            System.out.print(curr.val + \" \");\n            if (curr.left != null) queue.offer(curr.left);\n            if (curr.right != null) queue.offer(curr.right);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);\n        root.left = new TreeNode(2);\n        root.right = new TreeNode(3);\n        System.out.print(\"Inorder: \"); inOrder(root); System.out.println();\n        System.out.print(\"Level-Order: \"); levelOrder(root); System.out.println();\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>⚔️ Tree Traversals: DFS (Pre, In, Post) vs BFS (Level-Order)</h2>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>🎯 What We Are Learning & Why:</strong> <em>Master the four sacred traversal paths across binary trees: Pre-Order, In-Order, Post-Order, and Breadth-First Level-Order queue exploration.</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>Navigating every node in a tree requires systematic traversal strategies:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Pre-Order (Root &rarr; Left &rarr; Right):</strong> Used to clone, serialize, or copy a tree structure.</li>\n    <li><strong>In-Order (Left &rarr; Root &rarr; Right):</strong> In a Binary Search Tree (BST), In-Order traversal visits elements in <em>strictly sorted ascending order</em>!</li>\n    <li><strong>Post-Order (Left &rarr; Right &rarr; Root):</strong> Computes values bottom-up (deleting trees, computing subtree sizes or heights).</li>\n    <li><strong>Level-Order BFS:</strong> Traverses nodes layer by layer using a FIFO Queue.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Blueprint</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\nTree:      [ 1 ]\n            /     \\\n         [ 2 ]   [ 3 ]\n         /   \\\n      [ 4 ]  [ 5 ]\n\nDFS Pre-Order:   1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 5 -> 3\nDFS In-Order:    4 -> 2 -> 5 -> 1 -> 3\nDFS Post-Order:  4 -> 5 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1\nBFS Level-Order: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Battle Strategy & Arena Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>BST In-Order Signature:</strong> To verify if a tree is a valid BST, check if its In-Order traversal produces a strictly increasing sequence.</li>\n  <li><strong>Queue Level Boundary:</strong> In BFS, record queue.size() at the start of each while loop iteration to process nodes level by level.</li>\n  <li><strong>Complexity:</strong> All standard tree traversals run in O(N) time and O(H) auxiliary space where H is tree height.</li>\n</ul>"
