      {
        "title": "Binary Heap Mechanics: Array Representation",
        "language": "java",
        "starterCode": "public class HeapIndexing {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Complete binary tree stored in 0-indexed array\n        int[] heap = {10, 15, 30, 40, 50, 100, 40};\n        int i = 1; // Element 15\n        int parent = (i - 1) / 2;\n        int leftChild = 2 * i + 1;\n        int rightChild = 2 * i + 2;\n        System.out.println(\"Node: \" + heap[i] + \", Parent: \" + heap[parent] + \", Left: \" + heap[leftChild] + \", Right: \" + heap[rightChild]);\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>⚔️ Binary Heap Mechanics: Array Representation & Sift-Up/Down</h2>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>💡 The Teacher's Perspective:</strong><br />\n  Imagine managing an emergency room triage: you don't care about sorting all 500 waiting patients alphabetically. You only care about <strong>who is the single sickest patient right now</strong>! A <strong>Heap (Priority Queue)</strong> lets you extract the maximum (or minimum) element in O(1) time and insert new elements in O(log N) time!\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>🎯 What We Are Learning & Why:</strong> <em>A complete binary tree packed flat into a contiguous array! Understand heapify, sift-up, and sift-down mechanics that guarantee O(log N) priority updates.</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>A <strong>Binary Heap</strong> is a complete binary tree implemented with zero pointer nodes inside a simple contiguous array. Because the tree is perfectly filled from left to right at every level, child and parent indices are computed via arithmetic:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Left Child of <em>i</em>: <code>2 * i + 1</code></li>\n    <li>Right Child of <em>i</em>: <code>2 * i + 2</code></li>\n    <li>Parent of <em>i</em>: <code>(i - 1) / 2</code></li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>In a Min-Heap, the parent is always $\\le$ its children (root is the minimum). Inserting an element appends it to the end and performs <strong>sift-up</strong> (O(\\log N)). Extracting the minimum removes the root, swaps the last element into position 0, and performs <strong>sift-down</strong> (O(\\log N)).</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Blueprint</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\nBinary Heap Tree:              Array Representation:\n         [ 1 ]                   Index: 0  1  2  3  4  5\n        /     \\                  Value: [ 1, 3, 6, 5, 9, 8 ]\n     [ 3 ]   [ 6 ]\n     /   \\   /\n   [ 5 ] [ 9 ][ 8 ]\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Battle Strategy & Arena Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Build-Heap in O(N):</strong> Building a heap from an unsorted array takes O(N) time using bottom-up sift-down, NOT O(N log N)!</li>\n  <li><strong>Top Priority Lookup:</strong> Peeking at the minimum/maximum element takes strictly O(1) constant time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Java Implementation:</strong> In Java, PriorityQueue is a Min-Heap by default; pass Collections.reverseOrder() for a Max-Heap.</li>\n</ul>"
      },
      {
        "title": "The Top-K Elements Pattern & Min-Heap Eviction",
        "language": "java",
        "starterCode": "import java.util.PriorityQueue;\n\npublic class TopKDemo {\n    public static int findKthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {\n        // Min-Heap to maintain the k largest elements\n        PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();\n        for (int num : nums) {\n            minHeap.offer(num);\n            if (minHeap.size() > k) {\n                minHeap.poll(); // Evict smallest of the top candidates\n            }\n        }\n        return minHeap.peek();\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] nums = {3, 2, 1, 5, 6, 4};\n        System.out.println(\"3rd Largest: \" + findKthLargest(nums, 3));\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>⚔️ The Top-K Elements Pattern & Min-Heap Eviction</h2>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>🎯 What We Are Learning & Why:</strong> <em>Find the K largest elements among 100,000,000 items without sorting the entire array! The Min-Heap eviction pattern limits memory to strictly O(K).</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>If asked to find the <em>K</em> largest elements in a stream of size <em>N</em>, a naive sort costs O(N \\log N) time and O(N) space. When $N = 10^8$ and $K = 10$, sorting is unacceptably slow.</p>\n  <p>By maintaining a <strong>Min-Heap of size K</strong>, the heap root represents the <em>K</em>-th largest element seen so far. For every incoming number, if it is larger than the root, we pop the root and push the new number. Elements smaller than the root are discarded immediately. Runtime: O(N \\log K) with only O(K) space!</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Blueprint</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\nStream: [ 3, 2, 1, 5, 6, 4 ], K = 2 (Find 2 largest)\nHeap holds smallest of the top-2:\nInsert 3 -> [3]\nInsert 2 -> [2, 3] (size 2)\nInsert 1 -> 1 < min(2) -> ignore!\nInsert 5 -> 5 > min(2) -> poll 2, offer 5 -> [3, 5]\nInsert 6 -> 6 > min(3) -> poll 3, offer 6 -> [5, 6]\nInsert 4 -> 4 < min(5) -> ignore!\nResult: Top 2 elements are 5 and 6!\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Battle Strategy & Arena Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Min-Heap for K Largest:</strong> To find K LARGEST, use a MIN-heap (evict smallest). To find K SMALLEST, use a MAX-heap.</li>\n  <li><strong>QuickSelect Alternative:</strong> QuickSelect achieves average O(N) time for static arrays, but Heaps work seamlessly on streaming data.</li>\n  <li><strong>Classic Problems:</strong> Kth Largest Element in an Array (LeetCode 215), Top K Frequent Elements (LeetCode 347).</li>\n</ul>"
      },
      {
        "title": "Merge K Sorted Lists via Min-Heap Priority Queue",
        "language": "java",
        "starterCode": "import java.util.PriorityQueue;\n\npublic class MergeKLists {\n    public static ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {\n        if (lists == null || lists.length == 0) return null;\n        PriorityQueue<ListNode> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a.val - b.val);\n        for (ListNode node : lists) {\n            if (node != null) pq.offer(node);\n        }\n        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0), tail = dummy;\n        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {\n            ListNode minNode = pq.poll();\n            tail.next = minNode;\n            tail = tail.next;\n            if (minNode.next != null) pq.offer(minNode.next);\n        }\n        return dummy.next;\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>⚔️ Merge K Sorted Lists via Min-Heap Priority Queue</h2>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>🎯 What We Are Learning & Why:</strong> <em>Zip 1,000 sorted telemetry streams into a single unified stream in O(N log K) time using a capacity-bounded priority queue (LeetCode 23 Hard)!</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>When merging <em>K</em> sorted linked lists containing <em>N</em> total elements, naive sequential pairwise merging takes O(K \\cdot N) quadratic time.</p>\n  <p>Instead, initialize a Min-Heap containing the head node of each of the <em>K</em> lists. Extract the minimum node, append it to your output list, and immediately enqueue that node's <code>next</code> successor into the heap. Because the heap never contains more than <em>K</em> nodes, every extraction and insertion costs only O(\\log K) time, resulting in optimal O(N \\log K) performance!</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Blueprint</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\nList 1: [ 1 -> 4 -> 5 ]\nList 2: [ 1 -> 3 -> 4 ]\nList 3: [ 2 -> 6 ]\n\nMin-Heap of heads: [ 1 (L1), 1 (L2), 2 (L3) ]\nExtract 1 (L1) -> Output: 1 -> Offer 4 (L1)\nExtract 1 (L2) -> Output: 1 -> 1 -> Offer 3 (L2)\nExtract 2 (L3) -> Output: 1 -> 1 -> 2 -> Offer 6 (L3) ...\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Battle Strategy & Arena Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Comparator Contract:</strong> In Java: new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a.val, b.val)) to avoid subtraction underflows.</li>\n  <li><strong>Memory Neutrality:</strong> Reuses existing list nodes by rewiring next pointers; zero auxiliary list node objects allocated.</li>\n  <li><strong>Divide and Conquer Alternative:</strong> Pairwise divide-and-conquer merging also achieves O(N log K) without heap overhead.</li>\n</ul>"
      },
      {
        "title": "Two-Heaps Architecture: Finding Median from Data Stream",
        "language": "java",
        "starterCode": "import java.util.Collections;\nimport java.util.PriorityQueue;\n\npublic class MedianFinder {\n    private PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder()); // Small half\n    private PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(); // Large half\n\n    public void addNum(int num) {\n        maxHeap.offer(num);\n        minHeap.offer(maxHeap.poll());\n        if (maxHeap.size() < minHeap.size()) {\n            maxHeap.offer(minHeap.poll());\n        }\n    }\n\n    public double findMedian() {\n        if (maxHeap.size() > minHeap.size()) return maxHeap.peek();\n        return (maxHeap.peek() + minHeap.peek()) / 2.0;\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>⚔️ Two-Heaps Architecture: Finding Median from Data Stream</h2>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>🎯 What We Are Learning & Why:</strong> <em>Balance a Max-Heap against a Min-Heap to report the running median of a live data stream in strictly O(1) time with O(log N) insertions (LeetCode 295)!</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>Calculating the median of a dynamic stream is tricky: an array requires O(N) insertion shifts, while a balanced BST requires complex balancing. The <strong>Two-Heaps Pattern</strong> divides all incoming numbers into two equal halves:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>A <strong>Max-Heap</strong> storing the smaller half of numbers (peek gives largest of the small half).</li>\n    <li>A <strong>Min-Heap</strong> storing the larger half of numbers (peek gives smallest of the large half).</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>By keeping both heaps balanced in size (difference at most 1), the median is either the peek of the larger heap, or the average of both heap roots!</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Blueprint</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\n        [ Max-Heap: Small Half ]       [ Min-Heap: Large Half ]\n              [ 5 ]                         [ 8 ]\n             /     \\                       /     \\\n           [ 4 ]   [ 2 ]                 [ 10 ]  [ 12 ]\n              ^                             ^\n         peek(): 5                     peek(): 8\n\nMedian = (5 + 8) / 2.0 = 6.5 in strictly O(1) time!\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Battle Strategy & Arena Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Size Invariant:</strong> Enforce maxHeap.size() == minHeap.size() OR maxHeap.size() == minHeap.size() + 1.</li>\n  <li><strong>Value Invariant:</strong> Every element in maxHeap must be <= every element in minHeap. If violated, swap roots!</li>\n  <li><strong>Sliding Window Median:</strong> Extend this pattern with lazy node deletion to solve LeetCode 480 Sliding Window Median.</li>\n</ul>"
      },
      {
        "title": "Task Scheduler & Greedy Heap Strategies",
        "language": "java",
        "starterCode": "import java.util.*;\n\npublic class TaskScheduler {\n    public static int leastInterval(char[] tasks, int n) {\n        int[] counts = new int[26];\n        int maxCount = 0, maxCountTasks = 0;\n        for (char c : tasks) {\n            counts[c - 'A']++;\n            if (counts[c - 'A'] > maxCount) {\n                maxCount = counts[c - 'A'];\n                maxCountTasks = 1;\n            } else if (counts[c - 'A'] == maxCount) {\n                maxCountTasks++;\n            }\n        }\n        int partCount = maxCount - 1;\n        int partLength = n - (maxCountTasks - 1);\n        int emptySlots = partCount * partLength;\n        int availableTasks = tasks.length - maxCount * maxCountTasks;\n        int idles = Math.max(0, emptySlots - availableTasks);\n        return tasks.length + idles;\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>⚔️ Task Scheduler & Greedy Heap Strategies</h2>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>🎯 What We Are Learning & Why:</strong> <em>Tasks require a cooldown of N ticks between identical execution types. Minimize total CPU idle cycles using greedy frequency heaps (LeetCode 621)!</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>When scheduling tasks with identical execution runtimes and a fixed cooldown constraint <em>N</em>, the optimal greedy strategy is always to execute the <strong>most frequent remaining task</strong> first to prevent idle slots from bottlenecking the tail of the schedule.</p>\n  <p>We track task frequencies with a Max-Heap. In each cycle of length $N + 1$, we pop the top available tasks, decrement their remaining counts, and store them in a temporary waiting queue. At the end of the round, non-exhausted tasks are re-enqueued into the Max-Heap.</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Blueprint</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\nTasks: [ A, A, A, B, B, B ], Cooldown N = 2\nFrame Size = N + 1 = 3 slots\nRound 1: [ A ] -> [ B ] -> [ idle ]  (Counts left: A:2, B:2)\nRound 2: [ A ] -> [ B ] -> [ idle ]  (Counts left: A:1, B:1)\nRound 3: [ A ] -> [ B ]              (All tasks complete!)\nTotal Time = 3 + 3 + 2 = 8 intervals.\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Battle Strategy & Arena Invariants</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Mathematical Shortcut:</strong> Total time = Math.max(tasks.length, (maxFreq - 1) * (n + 1) + maxFreqCount).</li>\n  <li><strong>Reorganize String:</strong> Same greedy heap strategy solves LeetCode 767: arrange characters so no two adjacent are identical.</li>\n  <li><strong>Priority Queue Simulation:</strong> Pair Max-Heap with a FIFO wait-queue tracking {taskCount, availableTimeTick}.</li>\n</ul>"
      }
